1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,660
At the Corner Cafe, Asha finds the old
till code.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:07,020
Sam, who helped before her, wrote it in a
hurry.

3
00:00:07,067 --> 00:00:08,408
A function called f.

4
00:00:08,428 --> 00:00:10,245
Values called a, b and t 2.

5
00:00:10,265 --> 00:00:11,742
Numbers with no names.

6
00:00:11,762 --> 00:00:12,627
And no tests.

7
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:13,418
It works.

8
00:00:13,438 --> 00:00:18,048
But nobody can read it, and nobody dares
to change it.

9
00:00:18,653 --> 00:00:20,961
This is Code Quest, Chapter 27.

10
00:00:20,981 --> 00:00:25,993
Today, you read code someone else wrote,
and you improve it safely.

11
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:29,470
And an AI assistant will tidy some code
for Asha.

12
00:00:29,490 --> 00:00:32,715
It will say the behaviour is exactly the
same.

13
00:00:32,735 --> 00:00:34,267
Her tests will decide.

14
00:00:34,887 --> 00:00:40,176
Here is today's mission: Sam's till, with
clear names, named numbers and small

15
00:00:40,196 --> 00:00:40,857
functions.

16
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:44,724
It must give exactly the same results as
Sam's code.

17
00:00:44,744 --> 00:00:47,829
Let's get there, one small step at a time.

18
00:00:48,417 --> 00:00:54,317
Read unfamiliar code, write tests that
record what it does now, and refactor in

19
00:00:54,337 --> 00:00:55,217
small steps.

20
00:00:55,267 --> 00:00:59,286
Read a diff, and review an AI's changes
with your tests.

21
00:00:59,883 --> 00:01:05,030
Most programming work is changing code
that already exists, often written by

22
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:05,913
someone else.

23
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:11,126
Refactoring means changing the structure
of code, without changing what it does.

24
00:01:11,146 --> 00:01:15,556
And the proof is a set of tests, written
before you change anything.

25
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:19,370
Here is the big idea: a safe refactoring
loop.

26
00:01:19,390 --> 00:01:22,750
First, tests that record what the code
does now.

27
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:24,428
Then one small change.

28
00:01:24,448 --> 00:01:25,851
Then run the tests.

29
00:01:25,871 --> 00:01:26,825
Same results?

30
00:01:26,845 --> 00:01:27,424
Keep it.

31
00:01:27,444 --> 00:01:28,174
Different?

32
00:01:28,194 --> 00:01:28,773
Undo it.

33
00:01:28,793 --> 00:01:31,170
Then review, and go round again.

34
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,834
A test that records what the code does
now, even the odd parts, is called a

35
00:01:35,854 --> 00:01:37,199
characterization test.

36
00:01:37,219 --> 00:01:39,930
It does not ask whether the result is
right.

37
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:42,474
It asks whether it is the same as before.

38
00:01:43,087 --> 00:01:45,746
To review a change, you read a diff.

39
00:01:45,766 --> 00:01:49,467
A diff shows two versions of a file, line
by line.

40
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,399
A line that starts with minus was removed.

41
00:01:52,419 --> 00:01:55,110
A line that starts with plus was added.

42
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:58,376
A line that starts with a space did not
change.

43
00:01:58,987 --> 00:01:59,550
Breather.

44
00:01:59,570 --> 00:02:02,467
Tests first, then one small change at a
time.

45
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:05,017
Next, Asha reads Sam's code.

46
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:10,240
Step 1.

47
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:16,363
Before you change anything, read the code
from the first line, and say in words what

48
00:02:16,383 --> 00:02:17,456
each part does.

49
00:02:17,476 --> 00:02:20,590
Line 2 defines f, with two values, a and
b.

50
00:02:20,633 --> 00:02:22,679
Lines 4 and 5 go through a.

51
00:02:22,699 --> 00:02:27,730
Each x is a tuple, so x 1 and x 2 are its
second and third values.

52
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:30,943
So a is an order of name, price and count.

53
00:02:30,967 --> 00:02:35,138
Line 6 checks b, and line 7 takes 10
percent off.

54
00:02:35,158 --> 00:02:38,817
So b says whether the customer is a
member.

55
00:02:38,867 --> 00:02:41,785
Line 8 adds tax, and line 9 rounds.

56
00:02:41,805 --> 00:02:44,640
Lines 14 to 16 call f three times.

57
00:02:56,013 --> 00:02:57,641
Zero point zero.

58
00:02:57,661 --> 00:03:01,142
The loop never runs, so t stays 0.

59
00:03:01,162 --> 00:03:06,703
But 0 times 1.1 is a float, so round gives
0.0, not 0.

60
00:03:06,733 --> 00:03:11,712
Lines 14 and 15 print 12.38, and 11.14 for
a member.

61
00:03:11,732 --> 00:03:14,499
Remember these three results.

62
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:18,333
To check her reading, Asha traces one
input.

63
00:03:18,353 --> 00:03:23,110
Line 8 prints the running total inside the
loop, as in Chapter 9.

64
00:03:23,133 --> 00:03:27,818
On the first pass, two teas at 3.5 make
7.0.

65
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:42,956
Cake, 4.25, 1, t equals 11.25.

66
00:03:42,976 --> 00:03:45,360
7.0, plus one cake.

67
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,636
And with tax, 12.38.

68
00:03:47,656 --> 00:03:51,809
Asha's reading of the code was right.

69
00:03:52,383 --> 00:03:52,875
Step 2.

70
00:03:52,895 --> 00:03:56,824
Now Asha records what f does today, before
any change.

71
00:03:56,844 --> 00:04:00,773
Line 2 imports f, and line 4 is the order
from step 1.

72
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:03,568
Each test uses one result from step 1.

73
00:04:03,588 --> 00:04:09,290
The normal order, the member order, and
the odd one: an empty order gives 0.0.

74
00:04:09,333 --> 00:04:10,425
She runs pytest.

75
00:04:10,445 --> 00:04:12,163
Three dots, three passed.

76
00:04:12,183 --> 00:04:17,305
From now on, any change that breaks one of
them has changed the behaviour.

77
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:18,904
Quick check.

78
00:04:18,924 --> 00:04:23,169
Why does Asha's test expect 0.0 for an
empty order?

79
00:04:34,547 --> 00:04:34,678
C.

80
00:04:34,698 --> 00:04:38,852
A characterization test records what the
code does now.

81
00:04:38,872 --> 00:04:41,887
It is not a judgement of right or wrong.

82
00:04:42,483 --> 00:04:45,586
Step 3: the first small change, the names.

83
00:04:45,606 --> 00:04:50,343
F becomes order total, a becomes items,
and b becomes is member.

84
00:04:50,367 --> 00:04:56,493
Line 4 unpacks each tuple into name, price
and count, as in Chapter 13.

85
00:04:56,513 --> 00:04:58,657
So x 1 and x 2 disappear.

86
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:02,682
And t 2 becomes with tax, a name that says
what it holds.

87
00:05:03,287 --> 00:05:08,013
The tests change in one way only: line 2
imports the new name.

88
00:05:08,033 --> 00:05:11,458
Every expected value stays exactly as it
was.

89
00:05:22,813 --> 00:05:23,678
Three passed.

90
00:05:23,698 --> 00:05:26,740
The names changed, and the behaviour did
not.

91
00:05:27,317 --> 00:05:27,854
Step 4.

92
00:05:27,874 --> 00:05:33,342
A magic number is a number in the code
with no name, like 0.1 or 1.1.

93
00:05:33,362 --> 00:05:36,127
A reader cannot tell what it means.

94
00:05:36,167 --> 00:05:38,026
A constant gives it a name.

95
00:05:38,046 --> 00:05:43,596
By convention, constants are written in
capital letters, at the top of the file.

96
00:05:43,616 --> 00:05:48,260
Python will not stop a change, but the
capitals tell people not to.

97
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:50,627
Lines 11 and 12 now use the names.

98
00:05:50,667 --> 00:05:52,361
Then she runs the tests.

99
00:05:52,953 --> 00:05:56,096
Line 2 imports the version with constants.

100
00:05:56,116 --> 00:05:57,601
Three passed, again.

101
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:00,453
Now Asha reviews the change.

102
00:06:00,473 --> 00:06:06,812
The diff lib module compares till v 2,
from step 3, with till v 3, from step 4.

103
00:06:06,832 --> 00:06:11,560
Lines 7 and 8 skip the file comment on
line 1 of each file.

104
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:18,129
On line 9, v 2 and v 3 are the labels for
the two versions, and n equals 1 shows one

105
00:06:18,149 --> 00:06:20,780
unchanged line around each change.

106
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:24,100
The plus lines at the top add the two
constants.

107
00:06:24,133 --> 00:06:29,815
And these minus and plus pairs swap 0.1
and 1.1 for their names.

108
00:06:29,835 --> 00:06:32,488
Every other line is unchanged.

109
00:06:33,087 --> 00:06:33,842
Quick check.

110
00:06:33,862 --> 00:06:37,462
In a diff, what does a line that starts
with minus mean?

111
00:06:48,813 --> 00:06:48,961
B.

112
00:06:48,981 --> 00:06:50,222
It was removed.

113
00:06:50,242 --> 00:06:51,650
Plus means added.

114
00:06:52,253 --> 00:06:52,796
Breather.

115
00:06:52,816 --> 00:06:56,543
Two small changes done, and the tests
passed after each one.

116
00:06:56,567 --> 00:07:01,538
Next, the last step, and Sam's code side
by side with Asha's.

117
00:07:06,283 --> 00:07:08,158
Step 5 extracts a function.

118
00:07:08,178 --> 00:07:13,840
The loop that adds up the order becomes
its own function, subtotal, on lines 7 to

119
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:15,313
12, with a docstring.

120
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:17,081
Line 12 returns the total.

121
00:07:17,101 --> 00:07:21,023
Without that return, the result would
never come back out.

122
00:07:21,067 --> 00:07:26,074
Order total now calls subtotal on line 17,
and takes off the discount.

123
00:07:26,094 --> 00:07:32,107
The name with tax is no longer needed, so
the tax moves into the return, on line 20.

124
00:07:32,133 --> 00:07:35,110
And line 2 is a docstring for the whole
module.

125
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:39,209
The tests pass on the final version too:
three passed.

126
00:07:39,820 --> 00:07:44,675
To be sure, Asha runs Sam's f, and her
order total, side by side.

127
00:07:44,695 --> 00:07:50,450
Line 5 has three orders: the one from step
1, three juices, and an empty one.

128
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:54,923
Lines 6 and 7 try each order with and
without the member discount.

129
00:07:54,943 --> 00:07:59,905
Line 10 prints the old result, the new
result, and whether they are equal.

130
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:12,540
True, six times.

131
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:14,140
Every pair is equal.

132
00:08:14,167 --> 00:08:15,349
Mission complete!

133
00:08:15,369 --> 00:08:20,017
The code is easy to read, and it does
exactly what Sam's code did.

134
00:08:20,067 --> 00:08:23,692
But Sam left one more function, a stock
checker.

135
00:08:23,712 --> 00:08:28,705
Asha decides to tidy it with an AI
assistant, one stage at a time.

136
00:08:29,283 --> 00:08:32,801
This chapter's AI skill: a safe
refactoring loop.

137
00:08:32,821 --> 00:08:38,433
Tests first, then the AI's suggestion,
then your review, then the tests again.

138
00:08:38,467 --> 00:08:40,844
Check every claim with a real run.

139
00:08:40,864 --> 00:08:42,889
And never paste private data.

140
00:08:42,909 --> 00:08:44,722
Use made-up stock numbers.

141
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:46,396
Stage 1: plan.

142
00:08:46,416 --> 00:08:50,077
Sam's stock checker has the shortest names
yet.

143
00:08:50,097 --> 00:08:54,150
Asha asks the AI to explain it, without
changing it.

144
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:59,485
The AI says c h k returns the items that
will run out after the order.

145
00:08:59,505 --> 00:09:00,622
It sounds sure.

146
00:09:01,220 --> 00:09:03,600
Asha checks the claim with a run.

147
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:08,690
Line 6 calls c h k, and lines 7 and 8
print what is left of each item.

148
00:09:08,733 --> 00:09:09,888
Cake has 2 left.

149
00:09:09,908 --> 00:09:12,971
It has not run out, but it is in the list.

150
00:09:12,991 --> 00:09:15,833
And Scone, with exactly 3 left, is not.

151
00:09:15,867 --> 00:09:20,316
So c h k returns the items that will have
fewer than 3 left.

152
00:09:20,336 --> 00:09:23,445
The AI's explanation was close, but wrong.

153
00:09:24,053 --> 00:09:25,143
Stage 2: write.

154
00:09:25,163 --> 00:09:31,063
Asha writes her own characterization tests
first, with the results of real runs.

155
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:35,290
Exactly 3 left is the boundary, so it gets
its own test.

156
00:09:35,333 --> 00:09:40,919
And one test checks that the results keep
the order of the order: Scone, then Cake.

157
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:43,485
Here are her five tests.

158
00:09:43,505 --> 00:09:47,538
Line 12 expects an empty list for exactly
3 left.

159
00:09:47,558 --> 00:09:50,350
Line 21 expects Scone before Cake.

160
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:52,853
On Sam's code, all five pass.

161
00:09:53,453 --> 00:09:57,303
Then she asks the AI which inputs her
tests do not cover yet.

162
00:09:57,333 --> 00:10:00,851
It suggests an item that is not in the
stock.

163
00:10:00,871 --> 00:10:02,659
A fair idea, for later.

164
00:10:03,253 --> 00:10:04,673
Stage 3: generate.

165
00:10:04,693 --> 00:10:10,673
Asha asks for clear names, exactly the
same behaviour, and no new features.

166
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:15,559
The AI gives her low stock, with a
constant, and says the behaviour is

167
00:10:15,579 --> 00:10:16,744
exactly the same.

168
00:10:17,353 --> 00:10:23,505
Asha reads the change as a diff, this time
with n equals 0, so no unchanged lines.

169
00:10:23,525 --> 00:10:29,751
Every name changed, c h k, s, o, r and k,
so every line shows as changed: six minus

170
00:10:29,771 --> 00:10:31,783
lines, and nine plus lines.

171
00:10:31,833 --> 00:10:33,996
So she reads them in pairs.

172
00:10:34,016 --> 00:10:37,310
Look at the old if line, and the new one.

173
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:49,830
The comparison.

174
00:10:49,850 --> 00:10:52,171
The old line says less than 3.

175
00:10:52,191 --> 00:10:56,073
The new line says less than or equal to
LOW LIMIT.

176
00:10:56,093 --> 00:11:00,599
With less than or equal, exactly 3 left
now counts as low.

177
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:02,347
Stage 4: test.

178
00:11:02,367 --> 00:11:09,228
Line 2 imports the AI's function with as c
h k, like import math as m in Chapter 18.

179
00:11:09,248 --> 00:11:12,750
So the same five tests run on the new
code.

180
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:15,894
One failed: the test for exactly 3 left.

181
00:11:15,914 --> 00:11:20,021
The AI changed the behaviour, and it said
it had not.

182
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:26,020
She shows the AI the failing test, and
asks which line changed the behaviour.

183
00:11:26,067 --> 00:11:29,371
Now the AI names line 8, and the
comparison.

184
00:11:29,391 --> 00:11:31,486
Change it back to less than.

185
00:11:32,087 --> 00:11:37,628
Asha changes less than or equal back to
less than, on line 8, and keeps the clear

186
00:11:37,648 --> 00:11:38,040
names.

187
00:11:38,653 --> 00:11:40,727
Then she runs every test again.

188
00:11:40,747 --> 00:11:41,537
Five passed.

189
00:11:41,557 --> 00:11:46,534
A regression is a change that breaks
something that used to work, and this

190
00:11:46,554 --> 00:11:47,952
rerun checks for one.

191
00:11:48,553 --> 00:11:49,631
Stage 5: debug.

192
00:11:49,651 --> 00:11:52,485
Then Asha's daily report stops working.

193
00:11:52,505 --> 00:11:57,533
She pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

194
00:11:57,567 --> 00:11:59,390
Cannot import name c h k.

195
00:11:59,410 --> 00:12:01,971
Her report still uses the old name.

196
00:12:01,991 --> 00:12:07,576
The AI suggests keeping the old name as a
second name for the same function.

197
00:12:08,187 --> 00:12:14,100
A function is a value, like a number, so
line 14 can give it a second name, as the

198
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:15,909
as keyword did on import.

199
00:12:15,929 --> 00:12:19,527
Now c h k and low stock lead to the same
function.

200
00:12:19,567 --> 00:12:24,486
And line 6 is a docstring with the true
behaviour: fewer than 3 left.

201
00:12:25,087 --> 00:12:28,467
Her report imports c h k again, and it
runs.

202
00:12:29,087 --> 00:12:32,757
And all five tests still pass on the final
version.

203
00:12:32,777 --> 00:12:33,770
No regression.

204
00:12:34,387 --> 00:12:35,738
Stage 6: improve.

205
00:12:35,758 --> 00:12:40,657
Asha asks for one readability change that
keeps every result.

206
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:45,675
The AI suggests a comprehension, and
sorted, so the list is tidier.

207
00:12:57,047 --> 00:12:57,235
No.

208
00:12:57,255 --> 00:13:01,599
Sorted puts Cake before Scone, so the test
for the order fails.

209
00:13:01,619 --> 00:13:04,856
Sorting is a new feature that nobody asked
for.

210
00:13:05,453 --> 00:13:08,323
One failed: keeps order of the order.

211
00:13:08,367 --> 00:13:11,985
A refactoring that changes the output is
not a refactoring.

212
00:13:12,587 --> 00:13:18,511
So Asha keeps the part that helps, the
comprehension on lines 7 and 8, and drops

213
00:13:18,531 --> 00:13:19,031
sorted.

214
00:13:19,051 --> 00:13:21,484
Line 9 returns the list as it is.

215
00:13:22,087 --> 00:13:23,912
She runs every test again.

216
00:13:23,932 --> 00:13:24,764
Five passed.

217
00:13:24,784 --> 00:13:29,378
That is a real improvement: clearer code,
and the same behaviour.

218
00:13:29,987 --> 00:13:30,848
Quick check.

219
00:13:30,868 --> 00:13:34,302
An AI says its refactoring keeps the
behaviour.

220
00:13:34,322 --> 00:13:35,258
What decides?

221
00:13:46,613 --> 00:13:46,742
A.

222
00:13:46,762 --> 00:13:48,751
Your tests, and a real run.

223
00:13:48,771 --> 00:13:54,701
Today they caught a wrong explanation, a
changed comparison and a feature nobody

224
00:13:54,721 --> 00:13:55,445
asked for.

225
00:13:56,053 --> 00:13:56,676
Breather.

226
00:13:56,696 --> 00:14:01,533
You watched Asha catch three AI mistakes,
each with a run or a test.

227
00:14:01,567 --> 00:14:05,071
Next, four mistakes people make when they
refactor.

228
00:14:09,817 --> 00:14:10,515
Mistake 1.

229
00:14:10,535 --> 00:14:14,968
This tidy version rounds each line inside
the loop, on line 8.

230
00:14:14,988 --> 00:14:17,697
It passes all three tests from step 2.

231
00:14:17,733 --> 00:14:21,523
With two teas, both results are 11.14.

232
00:14:21,567 --> 00:14:24,821
But with one tea, they are 7.67 and 7.68.

233
00:14:24,841 --> 00:14:27,216
Test several different inputs.

234
00:14:27,236 --> 00:14:31,768
And never move rounding into a loop during
a refactoring.

235
00:14:32,387 --> 00:14:33,196
Mistake 2.

236
00:14:33,216 --> 00:14:36,346
The function has a new name on line 2.

237
00:14:36,366 --> 00:14:37,590
Look at line 9.

238
00:14:48,947 --> 00:14:50,839
It stops with a name error.

239
00:14:50,859 --> 00:14:54,877
F is not defined, because Python only
knows the new name.

240
00:14:54,900 --> 00:15:00,939
The fix: change every call, or keep the
old name as a second name, as Asha did

241
00:15:00,959 --> 00:15:01,794
with c h k.

242
00:15:02,387 --> 00:15:03,258
Mistake 3.

243
00:15:03,278 --> 00:15:07,626
Lines 2 to 5 are an extracted function,
subtotal.

244
00:15:07,646 --> 00:15:09,677
Line 9 uses its result.

245
00:15:21,047 --> 00:15:21,809
A return.

246
00:15:21,829 --> 00:15:28,507
Without it, subtotal gives back None, and
line 9 cannot multiply None by 1.1.

247
00:15:28,533 --> 00:15:33,019
The fix: return total, as on line 12 of
the finished till.

248
00:15:33,620 --> 00:15:34,573
Mistake 4.

249
00:15:34,593 --> 00:15:38,464
This version forgot the member discount.

250
00:15:38,484 --> 00:15:41,480
Old says 11.14, new says 12.38.

251
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:45,652
Changing the test to expect 12.38 would
hide the bug.

252
00:15:45,672 --> 00:15:49,588
Never change expected values during a
refactoring.

253
00:15:49,608 --> 00:15:51,477
Undo the change instead.

254
00:15:52,087 --> 00:15:52,624
Breather.

255
00:15:52,644 --> 00:15:56,027
Now you know the four most common
refactoring mistakes.

256
00:15:56,067 --> 00:15:59,404
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

257
00:16:04,150 --> 00:16:04,495
First.

258
00:16:04,515 --> 00:16:09,058
Sam's sales function adds up the day and
prints a report, in the same loop.

259
00:16:09,078 --> 00:16:12,830
How would you split it, and which tests
would you write first?

260
00:16:24,180 --> 00:16:26,613
Keep your answer for the practice tasks.

261
00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:27,661
Second.

262
00:16:27,681 --> 00:16:32,991
Sometimes refactoring is not worth it, for
example code that will be deleted next

263
00:16:33,011 --> 00:16:33,320
week.

264
00:16:33,340 --> 00:16:35,755
What would you ask before you decide?

265
00:16:47,113 --> 00:16:49,226
Think about it after the video.

266
00:16:49,817 --> 00:16:50,577
Let's recap.

267
00:16:50,597 --> 00:16:54,737
Read from the top, say what each part
does, and trace one input.

268
00:16:54,767 --> 00:16:58,385
A characterization test records what the
code does now.

269
00:16:58,405 --> 00:17:00,767
Write these tests before any change.

270
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:04,980
Refactor in small steps, and run the tests
after every step.

271
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,710
In a diff, minus was removed and plus was
added.

272
00:17:08,733 --> 00:17:14,079
And check every AI claim with a run, and
every AI change with your tests.

273
00:17:14,687 --> 00:17:15,716
One last check.

274
00:17:15,736 --> 00:17:18,165
During a refactoring, a test fails.

275
00:17:18,185 --> 00:17:19,215
What do you do?

276
00:17:30,580 --> 00:17:30,706
D.

277
00:17:30,726 --> 00:17:33,630
Undo the change, and try a smaller step.

278
00:17:33,650 --> 00:17:36,116
The test is telling you the truth.

279
00:17:36,720 --> 00:17:37,880
Quest complete!

280
